Here is a story about a teenage wood elf in a Hong Kong school.

---

My name is Ka-yan, but everyone calls me Yan. I am a Form 4 student at St. Margaret’s College in Kowloon. I am also a wood elf.

My secret is not easy to keep. In the forest, my skin is the colour of moss after rain, and my ears are long and pointed like the leaves of a bamboo. But here, in the concrete jungle of Hong Kong, I wear a mask. My skin is a careful spell, my ears are hidden under my long, straight hair. My mother, who came from the ancient woods of the New Territories, taught me this magic. "We survive by hiding," she said. "The city is not our home."

School is a battlefield of a different kind. The fluorescent lights in the classroom are a dull, humming pain, like a headache that never goes away. The air is thick with the smell of markers and instant noodles, not the clean scent of earth and rain. My classmates, the "mortals" as I call them in my head, are obsessed with things I don't understand: the latest K-pop dance, the best bubble tea shop, who is dating who.

My only friend is a boy named Leo. He sits next to me in Biology. He is quiet and kind, and he draws pictures of trees in his notebook. He doesn't know my secret, but he seems to sense something. "Yan," he whispered once, during a boring lesson on photosynthesis. "You smell like the forest after a typhoon. It's nice."

I almost cried. I wanted to tell him that the forest is my blood, my breath. That I can feel the pain of a tree being cut down in the park across the street. That the little potted plant on the teacher's desk sings to me in a language of green and growth. But I just smiled and said, "It's my shampoo."

The hardest part is the PE class. We have to run around the hot, dusty track. My body is built for silent, graceful movement through the undergrowth, not for pounding on hard concrete. My feet ache for the soft earth. The other girls laugh at me because I am slow and clumsy. They don't know that I can climb a banyan tree faster than a monkey, or that I can walk on fallen leaves without making a sound.

One day, during a hiking trip for Geography, we went to a country park. The moment my feet touched the soil, a wave of energy washed over me. The trees whispered my name. The birds sang a welcome. I felt my skin tingle, the spell weakening. I had to run away from the group, pretending I needed the toilet. I found a hidden grove of old camphor trees. I took off my shoes and let my toes sink into the damp earth. I let my hair down and felt my ears stretch, just a little, towards the sun. For ten minutes, I was myself. I was a wood elf.

When I came back, Leo was waiting for me. "You look different," he said, his eyes wide. "Happier. More... alive."

I just shrugged. "The air is better here."

He didn't push. He just smiled and pointed to a small, perfect fern growing by the path. "Look," he said. "It's beautiful."

And in that moment, I didn't feel so alone. Maybe the city wasn't my home, but maybe, just maybe, I could find a little piece of the forest in a friend who saw the beauty in a single fern. For now, that was enough. [end of text]


